  "prediction": "The report provides an overview of the personal exemption amounts, standard deductions, and statutory marginal tax rates schedules for each tax year from 1988 to 2019. The report also discusses the shift from using the Consumer Price Index (CPI) to the chained Consumer Price Index (chained CPI) for inflation adjustments in tax elements. The chained CPI is designed to better capture changes in consumer spending patterns tied to price increases or decreases. However, using the chained CPI for inflation adjustments is likely to result in more bracket creep and an increase in federal tax revenue over time. The report also notes that several other federal programs, such as Social Security, have experienced similar situations with no cost-of-living adjustments during periods of deflation. By law, income tax elements cannot fall below their base-year values. Overall, the report highlights the importance of understanding the impact of inflation adjustments on tax elements and the potential implications for taxpayers.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides an overview of the personal exemption amounts, standard deductions, and statutory marginal tax rates schedules for each tax year from 1988 to 2019. The report also discusses the shift from using the Consumer Price Index (CPI) to the chained Consumer Price Index (chained CPI) for inflation adjustments in tax elements. The chained CPI is designed to better capture changes in consumer spending patterns tied to price increases or decreases. However, using the chained CPI for inflation adjustments is likely to result in more bracket creep and an increase in federal tax revenue over time. The report also notes that several other federal programs, such as Social Security, have experienced similar situations with no cost-of-living adjustments during periods of deflation. By law, income tax elements cannot fall below their base-year values. Overall, the report highlights the importance of understanding the impact of inflation adjustments on tax elements and the potential implications for taxpayers.",
